class.description:
A playback keeps track of the [SubtitleSegment]s that are active at a time. Pass it a time with [method update] (every frame) or [method seek] (a jump). It emits [signal segment_ended], [signal segment_started] and [signal segment_changed], in this order, whenever the active segments change. While nothing changes, it emits nothing and does not allocate memory.
[SubtitlePlayer] drives a playback from a scene. A playback also works on its own, without a scene and without threads, for example in tools, in tests or with a clock of your own:
[codeblocks]
[gdscript]
var playback = SubtitlePlayback.new()
var time = 0.0

func _ready():
    playback.subtitle = load("res://intro.srt")
    playback.segment_changed.connect(_on_segment_changed)

func _process(delta):
    time += delta
    playback.update(time)

func _on_segment_changed(segment):
    $SubtitleLabel.text = segment.text if segment else ""
[/gdscript]
[csharp]
private readonly SubtitlePlayback _playback = new SubtitlePlayback();
private double _time;

public override void _Ready()
{
    _playback.Subtitle = GD.Load<Subtitle>("res://intro.srt");
    _playback.SegmentChanged += segment => GetNode<Label>("SubtitleLabel").Text = segment?.Text ?? "";
}

public override void _Process(double delta)
{
    _time += delta;
    _playback.Update(_time);
}
[/csharp]
[/codeblocks]
Times are playback times: the times of the segments plus [member Subtitle.time_offset], as in [method Subtitle.get_segments_at]. Finding the active segments costs a binary search over the segments sorted by start time. The segments need not be sorted in the [Subtitle].
[b]Note:[/b] The playback reports all segments. It does not check [member SubtitleServer.enabled] or [member SubtitleServer.captions_enabled]. To get the text to show for a segment, use [method SubtitleServer.get_display_text].
The playback reads the segment times again whenever the [Subtitle] emits [signal Resource.changed], which includes changes to its [SubtitleSegment]s.
